comments[1]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-08-01T20:21:51.948Z","harness:codex","Long-horizon agent-environment plan 2026-08-01: pm supplies a framework-neutral substrate, not a training framework. Phase A stabilizes episode identity, deterministic reset, action and observation schemas, verdict predicates, trajectory projection, and point-in-time replay. Phase B adds bounded observation costs, attestation, dataset export, seeded fault and concurrency simulation, and reproducibility across OS and process boundaries. Phase C scales episodes across 100k and 1M-item corpora and thousand-agent fleets with resumable streams, content-addressed shared observations, privacy-safe provenance, and contamination controls. Phase D publishes environment and benchmark authoring kits, offline evaluation adapters, sim-to-real validation hooks, and domain packs. Every phase stays SDK-only, versioned, replayable, and auditable; reward shaping, model policy, and framework-specific orchestration remain external."
notes[2]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-08-14T05:39:35.452Z","harness:claude-code","Programme note 2026-08-14 — where simulation-to-real transfer sits, and why it is not a fifth seam.\n\nThis epic has four seams and takes no more: pm-gd6vnn declares what an agent may observe, pm-e1vkee declares how an outcome is scored, pm-li2fj5 composes those into a reproducible episode, and pm-t8a0x1 is the runnable proof that all three are reachable from published SDK surface alone. Simulation-to-real transfer, which is the remaining workflow named for this programme, is a property of how those four are configured, not a fifth thing to build.\n\n## The property, stated so it can be checked\n\nAn environment supports transfer when an episode recorded against a simulated workspace and an episode recorded against a real one are the same object, differing only in which workspace the recipe pointed at. Transfer fails when the simulated episode is cheaper, more deterministic, or more observable than the real one in ways the episode specification does not record, because then a policy trained on the simulation is fitted to the discrepancy.\n\nThree discrepancies are already visible in this project's own record and each is already owned.\n\nThe observation ceiling differs. pm-gd6vnn requires an observation to declare both a guaranteed ceiling and a workspace-calibrated expected cost. A three-item simulated workspace and a two-thousand-item real one have the same ceiling and wildly different expected cost, which is exactly the population-adequacy defect pm-s2h0mq measured on the read surfaces. Until expected cost is calibrated per workspace, a simulated episode understates what the real read will cost, and the agent is trained against an observation it will not receive.\n\nThe verdict differs by more than the workspace. pm-e1vkee's predicates are computed over recorded state, and a simulated workspace records a state its predicates can always resolve. The finding filed today as pm-xmmafu is the general case: a predicate over a count key that the producer discards when the count is zero resolves on a corpus with defects and stops resolving on a clean one. A simulation is usually the clean one. A reward function that silently stops resolving is worse than one that returns zero.\n\nThe step boundary differs. pm-a8lnoh's seeded interleaving harness makes fault injection reproducible, which is the correct substrate for the simulated half. What it does not yet do is state which of the injected faults occur in the real record and at what rate, so a policy can be trained against an interleaving distribution the production system never produces.\n\n## What follows, and what does not\n\nNothing here is a new item. Each discrepancy is a criterion belonging to a seam that already exists: expected-cost calibration to pm-gd6vnn, resolvability of predicates on a defect-free corpus to pm-e1vkee, and injected-fault rate fidelity to pm-a8lnoh. The right move when any of the four seams is next opened is to add the corresponding criterion there rather than to model transfer as its own deliverable.\n\nThe one thing that would be a new seam, and is deliberately not proposed, is a simulator. This programme's claim is that pm is the substrate and the verifier, not that it ships an environment. A simulated workspace is a real workspace with a different recipe, and if that ever stops being true the claim has been lost rather than extended."
  "2026-08-14T10:20:07.801Z","harness:codex","Agent-environment long-range architecture. An episode is a versioned recipe plus content-addressed workspace snapshot, deterministic seed, declared tool and network policy, bounded observation contract, action API, verifier set, reward decomposition, termination rule, and immutable provenance. The SDK must support reset, observe, act, checkpoint, fork, replay, score, and export without requiring the CLI. Trajectories stream observation-action-outcome records with durable cursors and stable episode or agent identities, while secrets and privileged context are redacted before training export. Evaluation separates task success, safety, efficiency, context quality, collaboration, and proof integrity so a scalar reward never hides regressions. The program should support single-agent and multi-agent episodes, offline evaluation, behavior cloning, RL, curriculum generation, adversarial benchmarks, sim-to-real comparisons, counterfactual replay, and human adjudication. Benchmark versions freeze environment, dataset, verifier, and scoring contracts; leakage controls separate training and held-out tasks; uncertainty and inter-rater disagreement remain first-class. Small local fixtures must run in seconds, while fleet orchestration shards episodes, deduplicates snapshots, and aggregates results without changing episode semantics."
body: ""
